WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.233
Every coding agent runs this loop.

00:00:02.233 --> 00:00:05.501
The model asks for a tool, the harness runs it, the

00:00:05.501 --> 00:00:06.689
result goes back in.

00:00:07.069 --> 00:00:09.046
Everything outside the model is the harness.

00:00:09.046 --> 00:00:12.435
So what happens if you keep the model and swap the harness?

00:00:14.623 --> 00:00:15.668
Hi, I'm Nib.

00:00:15.668 --> 00:00:19.501
I ran four open source coding harnesses against one local model

00:00:19.501 --> 00:00:20.894
and counted every token.

00:00:22.725 --> 00:00:26.231
Thirteen small repositories, each with a test suite that fails until

00:00:26.231 --> 00:00:27.506
the bug is fixed.

00:00:27.906 --> 00:00:31.830
Each harness gets the same prompt, twice per task, inside a sandbox.

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:36.379
A proxy in the middle forces the same sampling and logs every request.

00:00:36.779 --> 00:00:41.365
llama.cpp serves it, and reuses any prompt prefix it has already seen.

00:00:41.765 --> 00:00:45.191
And one model behind all of it, quantised to fit a

00:00:45.191 --> 00:00:46.437
single twenty-four gigabyte card.

00:00:48.058 --> 00:00:49.650
Twenty six runs per harness.

00:00:51.058 --> 00:00:52.863
And almost all of them pass.

00:00:52.863 --> 00:00:54.668
pi and OpenCode solve every task.

00:00:54.668 --> 00:00:56.473
The other two miss one each.

00:00:58.158 --> 00:01:00.402
The difference starts before any history.

00:01:00.402 --> 00:01:04.517
OpenCode's first request carries ten tools and a long system prompt.

00:01:04.517 --> 00:01:05.640
pi's carries four.

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:10.243
That prefix rides along on every request, so a whole run grows to match.

00:01:10.663 --> 00:01:14.320
OpenCode sends about four times pi's prompt for the same fixes.

00:01:15.758 --> 00:01:17.889
Locally the cache hides most of it.

00:01:17.889 --> 00:01:20.021
On a metered API it does not.

00:01:20.021 --> 00:01:24.283
Price cache reads at a tenth, and OpenCode costs three times what pi does.

00:01:25.858 --> 00:01:27.004
Aider has no tools.

00:01:27.004 --> 00:01:31.015
The model reads a map of the repository and names the files it wants.

00:01:31.435 --> 00:01:35.507
In the second request it writes search and replace blocks, and Aider applies them.

00:01:35.927 --> 00:01:37.484
Fewest tokens of all four.

00:01:37.484 --> 00:01:40.908
But the file contents arrive late, so only half its prompt

00:01:40.908 --> 00:01:42.154
is reused from cache.

00:01:43.758 --> 00:01:46.126
The most expensive run was a failure.

00:01:46.126 --> 00:01:50.862
mini-swe-agent went round sixty times on one task, two thirds of everything it sent.

00:01:50.862 --> 00:01:52.892
Only the step limit stopped it.

00:01:54.575 --> 00:01:57.354
On harder benchmarks the harness moves the score too.

00:01:57.354 --> 00:02:00.751
Here, on one model, a searched harness beats Claude Code's own.

00:02:02.475 --> 00:02:05.206
On work a strong model can do, the harness sets the

00:02:05.206 --> 00:02:06.199
bill, not the score.

00:02:06.199 --> 00:02:09.179
Look at what it sends every time, and at what stops it.

00:02:10.525 --> 00:02:13.862
Same model, same tasks, a four times spread in tokens.

00:02:13.862 --> 00:02:14.863
Thanks for watching!

00:02:15.283 --> 00:02:17.236
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:17.236 --> 00:02:17.794
I'm Nib.

00:02:17.794 --> 00:02:18.073
Bye!
